## How it works

The step screen of [`get_ab_steps`](/docs/tools/get-ab-steps) tests every cell
`(event, step)`: a cell is tested when its pooled support (paths with the event
at the step, and paths without it) reaches `min_support`, and the family is
every tested cell. This getter runs `get_ab_steps` with the same arguments and
projects every cell to `scale` times the current paths, as
[`get_ab_transitions_power`](/docs/tools/get-ab-transitions-power) does for the
edges: the projected support decides whether a cell passes the support rule
(`supported`), the cells that pass it at a scale form its family `m`, and the
`mde` is the cell's pooled z-test MDE at the projected arm sizes and the
Bonferroni level `alpha / m` (conservative for Benjamini-Hochberg). Every cell is
a 0/1 per-path share, so its spread is known at any size (`inference` is
`"projectable"` for a rare cell; a cell that every path has, or none, is
`"no_spread"` and never testable), and its MDE falls as `1 / sqrt(scale)` while
the family stays the same. At `scale=1` every number is the getter's. With
`target_delta`, the paths a cell needs are totals, solved with the family
projected at that size (`sizing_basis = "projected_family"`), and a path is one
`path_col`.

On `load_ab_test()` with `max_steps=8`, all 93 cells are testable and their
median MDE is 1.67 pp. A quarter of the users would leave 3 cells untested and
double the median MDE (3.40 pp); with `target_delta=0.01`, 14 % of the cells can
detect a 1 pp effect now, 60 % with four times the users and 98 % with sixteen.

The step screen usually needs fewer users than the transition screen of the same
stream: a path has one event per step, so a step's cells split the paths over
the vocabulary only, where the edges split them over its pairs. Its cells thin
out at later steps instead, as the paths end. The projection assumes the same
path composition, and sizes a new test; it is not a reason to run this one
longer. The [A/B Steps](/docs/widgets/ab-steps) widget draws it in its **Data
needed** view.

